   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to definitions related to\naddiction disorders, to change the name of the office of alcoholism and\nsubstance abuse services, and the scope of responsibilities of such office;\nand to repeal certain provisions of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S6591",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6591-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §1.03 subs 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 18, 25, 29, 40, 41, 43, 44, 45 & 57, amd §§1.03, 5.01, 5.03 & 19.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6591\n\nSPONSOR: HARCKHAM\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\ndefinitions related to addiction disorders, to change the name of the\noffice of alcoholism and substance abuse services, and the scope of\nresponsibilities of such office; and to repeal certain provisions of\nsuch law relating thereto\n\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law in relation to removing outdated\nand demeaning definitions related to addiction disorders, to change the\nname of the Office to de-stigmatize the disease of addiction, and to\nclarify the scope of Office responsibilities.\n\n \nSUMMARY:\n\nSections 1-8: Section 1.03 of the mental hygiene law (Definitions) is\namended to delete or amend certain definitions which are outdated,\nunnecessary, repetitive or demeaning to persons suffering from the\naddictive disorders of substance use disorder and gambling disorder;\nsuch terms include \"alcoholism,\" \"alcoholic,\" \"alcoholic beverage,\"\n\"recovered alcoholic,\" \"alcohol abuse,\" variations on the use of\n\"substance\" or \"chemical dependence,\" gambling disorder as an \"impulse\ncontrol disorder,\" and others. Section 1.03 also amended to conform the\ndefinition of Commissioner to correspond to the change in agency name\nproposed in section 9 of this bill.\n\nSection 9: The proposal amends section 1.03 of the mental hygiene law to\ndirect that any and all references to the Office of Alcoholism and\nSubstance Abuse Services and its predecessor agencies, subsequent to the\neffective date of the act be hereafter known as the \"Office of Addiction\nServices and Supports\" keeping the OASAS acronym but removing the stig-\nmatizing terms of \"alcoholism\" and \"substance abuse.\"\n\nSection 10: Amends section 5.01 of the mental hygiene law regarding the\ndefinition of the Department of Mental Hygiene to change the name of the\noffice as proposed in section 9 of this bill.\n\nSection 11: Amends section 5.03 of the mental hygiene law identifying\nthe commissioners in the Department of Mental Hygiene to correspond to\nthe change in the name of the office as proposed in section 9 of this\nbill.\n\nSection 12: Amends subdivisions (b) and (c) of section 19.07 of the\nmental hygiene law to clarify the scope of Office responsibilities\nregarding provision of addiction services.\n\nSection 13: Makes this act effective immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis proposal updates statute to reflect language usage responding to\nmedical and social developments and understanding about the nature of\naddictive disorders. The DMS-V has eliminated the use of such terms as\n\"alcoholism\"; federal CMS is adopting DSM-V more inclusive term of\n\"addictive disorder.\" Treatment professionals are reframing their clin-\nical standards with language that is accepting of addiction disorders as\nbrain function disorders, correcting years of stigmatization of these\ndisorders as personal or moral failings or a lack of impulse control.\nOASAS facilities are no longer \"alcoholism facilities\"; they are centers\nfor the specialized treatment of addiction disorders, including gambling\nand substance use.  This proposal corrects this language usage wherever\npossible, while being mindful of the multiple cross references in stat-\nute to existing definitions.\n\nSimilar to the office of people with developmental disabilities agency\nname change in 2010, the agency name should be updated to remove offen-\nsive language from the title and purpose. This is indicative of the\ngrowing consensus to treat substance use disorder and gambling disorder\nas chronic conditions. The Office also oversees the provision of gambl-\ning services which are not represented by the current agency name and\nwould be included in the new name of \"Office of Addiction Services and\nSupports.\"\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
